-- Q5458: Estate and Gift Tax [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 2702, a GRAT allows the grantor to retain annuity payments while transferring the remainder to beneficiaries. When trust assets appreciate above the Section 7520 hurdle rate, the excess growth passes to the children free of gift and estate tax. A zeroed-out GRAT preserves the lifetime exemption while shifting appreciation.
Wrong (B): An outright gift freezes value at today''s FMV but consumes $4,000,000 of exemption, whereas the GRAT achieves the same freeze with minimal exemption usage.
Wrong (C): The strategies do not produce identical results. The GRAT shifts excess appreciation tax-free, while the outright gift uses substantial exemption.
Wrong (D): In a properly structured zeroed-out GRAT, only the remainder (excess appreciation) passes outside the estate. The annuity payments return the original value to the grantor.$EXPL$
