-- Q5479: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1014(b)(6), when community property is included in a decedent''s gross estate, both the decedent''s half and the surviving spouse''s half receive a stepped-up basis to fair market value at death. Warren''s total basis becomes $900,000 ($450,000 for each half).
Wrong (A): $200,000 is the original cost basis. Section 1014(b)(6) provides a full step-up for both halves of community property, replacing the historical cost.
Wrong (B): $550,000 applies a step-up only to the decedent''s half, which is the rule for jointly held property in common-law states, not community property.
Wrong (D): $450,000 reflects only the decedent''s half at FMV. The community property rule grants a double step-up covering both halves.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5479;

-- Q5480: Estate and Gift Tax [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 2518, a qualified disclaimer allows Simmons to redirect $6,000,000 to a bypass trust utilizing Hutchins''s applicable exclusion amount. Without the disclaimer, the entire $20,000,000 would be in Simmons''s estate (totaling $30,000,000), exposing significantly more to estate tax at her death.
Wrong (A): While the marital deduction eliminates tax on Hutchins''s estate, it merely defers the tax to Simmons''s estate. The disclaimer shelters funds using Hutchins''s unused exemption.
Wrong (C): A qualified disclaimer is not treated as a gift. The property passes as though the disclaimant predeceased the decedent under Section 2518.
Wrong (D): A surviving spouse may disclaim property passing under a will. Section 2518 permits any person with an interest to make a qualified disclaimer.$EXPL$
