-- Q5501: Estate and Gift Tax [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 2511 and Treas. Reg. 25.2511-2, a gift is not complete until the donor relinquishes dominion and control. Because Spencer retained the power to change beneficiaries, no completed gift occurred at funding. When Spencer releases that power, the gift becomes complete and is valued at the trust''s $3,800,000 FMV on the release date.
Wrong (A): Retaining control delays the gift but does not make it tax-free. Upon release of the power, gift tax consequences arise.
Wrong (C): Gift tax valuation occurs when dominion and control are fully relinquished, not at the original funding date. The $3,800,000 value controls.
Wrong (D): An irrevocable trust does not automatically exclude assets from transfer taxation. If the donor retains and then releases powers, gift tax applies upon release.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5501;

-- Q5502: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 2001(b), the estate tax is computed on the taxable estate plus adjusted taxable gifts. The tentative tax on this combined amount is then reduced by the gift tax that would have been payable on lifetime gifts, preventing actual double taxation while applying progressive rates.
Wrong (B): Section 2001 requires adjusted taxable gifts to be added back. The gifts are not ignored merely because the unified credit covered them during life.
Wrong (C): Adjusted taxable gifts do not reduce the taxable estate. They are added to it to compute the tentative tax under the cumulative progressive rate structure.
Wrong (D): Lifetime gifts are not included in the gross estate (unless subject to special inclusion rules). They are added only as adjusted taxable gifts for rate computation purposes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5502;

-- Q5504: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 2612(b), a taxable distribution occurs when a distribution is made from a trust to a skip person while a non-skip person retains an interest. Because Kimball (non-skip) still holds an income interest, the $150,000 distribution to Manning (skip person) is a taxable distribution.
Wrong (A): A taxable termination under Section 2612(a) requires that all interests held by non-skip persons terminate. Kimball still retains an interest, so this is not a termination.
Wrong (B): Distributions to grandchildren are not automatically exempt from GST tax. Chapter 13 applies whenever property passes to a skip person.
Wrong (C): A direct skip requires a transfer directly from the transferor to a skip person, not a distribution from an existing trust to a skip person while non-skip persons hold interests.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5504;

