-- Q5513: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 2503(b)(2), the annual exclusion is adjusted for inflation in $1,000 increments using the Consumer Price Index, rounded down. Because the statute rounds down rather than adjusting continuously, the exclusion can only increase when inflation accumulates a full $1,000 increment -- it never decreases.
Wrong (B): The exclusion does not reset to $10,000 upon legislative amendment. The base amount adjusts upward through the inflation indexing mechanism.
Wrong (C): The rounding-down mechanism prevents downward adjustments even if the CPI temporarily declines. The exclusion remains at the last rounded amount.
Wrong (D): The exclusion is not permanently fixed at $19,000. It continues to be indexed and will increase when cumulative inflation supports the next $1,000 increment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 5513;

-- Q5514: Estate and Gift Tax [medium/L3]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 2042, life insurance proceeds are included in the insured''s gross estate if the insured possesses any incidents of ownership at death, such as the power to change beneficiaries, borrow against, or surrender the policy. The ILIT must own all incidents from inception to achieve exclusion.
Wrong (A): Naming a spouse as trust beneficiary does not cause inclusion under Section 2042. Incidents of ownership, not beneficiary designation of the trust, trigger inclusion.
Wrong (B): Premium payments by the insured do not by themselves create incidents of ownership. The IRS treats them as gifts to the trust, not evidence of policy ownership.
Wrong (D): The three-year rule under Section 2035 applies to transfers of existing policies, not to policies purchased by and owned by the trust from inception.$EXPL$
